OLLAMA & LLM ENGINE REFERENCE — BBEA DEPLOYMENT GUIDE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. OLLAMA (Primary — Local Inference)
──────────────────────────────────────
  What:     Local LLM runtime. Runs GGUF-quantized models on consumer GPUs.
  Install:  curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
  Models:   llama3.1:8b, llama3.1:70b, mistral, codellama, phi3
  API:      REST on localhost:11434
  Use case: Development, local testing, air-gapped deployment

  Key commands:
    ollama pull llama3.1:8b          # download base model
    ollama create bbea -f Modelfile  # create custom model from Modelfile
    ollama run bbea                  # interactive chat
    ollama serve                     # start API server (background)

  API call (from Java / curl):
    POST http://localhost:11434/api/generate
    {"model":"bbea","prompt":"<input>","stream":false}

2. vLLM (Production — High-Throughput Serving)
──────────────────────────────────────────────
  What:     High-performance inference engine with PagedAttention.
  Install:  pip install vllm
  Use case: Production API, multiple concurrent users, batched requests
  Hardware: Requires CUDA GPU (A100/H100 for 70B models)

  Launch:
    python -m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server \
      --model meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct \
      --tensor-parallel-size 2

  API: OpenAI-compatible endpoint on port 8000

3. llama.cpp (Quantization & Edge Deployment)
─────────────────────────────────────────────
  What:     C++ inference for GGUF models. CPU or GPU. Minimal dependencies.
  Install:  git clone https://github.com/ggerganov/llama.cpp && make
  Use case: Edge deployment, Raspberry Pi, quantization pipeline

  Quantize merged LoRA adapter:
    python convert_hf_to_gguf.py ./merged-model --outtype q4_k_m

4. Unsloth (Fine-Tuning — Fast LoRA)
─────────────────────────────────────
  What:     2x faster LoRA/QLoRA fine-tuning for Llama, Mistral, Phi.
  Install:  pip install unsloth
  Use case: Training BBEA adapter on dataset.us.jsonl

  Example:
    from unsloth import FastLanguageModel
    model, tokenizer = FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained("unsloth/llama-3.1-8b-bnb-4bit")
    model = FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(model, r=16, lora_alpha=32,
              target_modules=["q_proj","v_proj","k_proj","o_proj"])
    # ... train with SFTTrainer on dataset.us.jsonl ...
    model.save_pretrained_merged("bbea-merged", tokenizer)

5. Axolotl (Fine-Tuning — Config-Driven)
─────────────────────────────────────────
  What:     YAML-config-driven fine-tuning framework. Supports LoRA, full-FT.
  Install:  pip install axolotl
  Use case: Reproducible training runs, multi-GPU setups

  Config snippet (axolotl.yml):
    base_model: meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct
    adapter: lora
    lora_r: 16
    lora_alpha: 32
    datasets:
      - path: ./dataset.us.jsonl
        type: sharegpt
    sequence_len: 4096
    num_epochs: 3
    micro_batch_size: 4
    learning_rate: 0.0002

6. INTEGRATION WITH N21 SERVER
──────────────────────────────
  The AIProctorModule (source/ai/AIProctorModule.java, port 49111) connects
  to Ollama's REST API to run BBEA evaluations triggered by:
    • New connection with National ID (auto-profile assessment)
    • Admin-initiated audit commands via ModuleInstallationService
    • Scheduled batch audits via Communicator's message scheduler

  Flow:
    Client → telnet 49111 → AIProctorModule
    → HTTP POST localhost:11434/api/generate {"model":"bbea",...}
    → Parse JSON response → Store in N21 DB (national_finance_ids)
    → Return audit report to client
